  abstract: |
    Machines that improve themselves using their own outputs now exist and work, and there is no accepted science of what limits them. This paper proposes that science, Recursive Dynamics, and stakes its claim the way thermodynamics was staked in 1824: by computing a bound that any such machine must obey if the proposal is right, publishing the instruments that measure its terms, and preregistering by timestamp the experiments whose nulls favour the rival view. The field takes the self-improvement loop as its native object and proposes five substrate-independent state variables: capability, recursive depth, correction rate, drift and correction leverage. It carries five laws as named conjectures with statuses declared. One, the ARC Ceiling, is derived in full from two lines of assumption; two, added on 24 September 2026, have their mathematics set out at version 1.0 and are neither registered as laws nor tested. The field locates its identity, as thermodynamics does, in its impossibilities: if the laws hold, self-improvement cannot outrun its own correction, the growth exponent is capped by the reciprocal of the correction shortfall, and no ladder of same-class oversight climbs past its ceiling by adding rungs. The paper states what would make the field wrong, tries to house its questions inside twelve neighbouring fields, gives each of fifty-one objections a disposition, and prints the four results that would dissolve the field. Its honest status is the Carnot stage: one memoir, instruments built, decisive measurements preregistered by timestamp and not run, staked where failure will be as visible as success.

    Keywords: Recursive Dynamics; recursive self-improvement; the ARC Theory; correction and drift; the ARC Ceiling; limit theorems; AI alignment; preregistration

